1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:03,260
Welcome to today's deep dive. Um, I want you to imagine

2
00:00:03,880 --> 00:00:06,560
dumping a thousand blank puzzle pieces onto a table.

3
00:00:07,240 --> 00:00:09,700
You can see every single cardboard fragment, but,

4
00:00:10,540 --> 00:00:13,860
uh, without a picture to guide you, they explain absolutely nothing.

5
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:15,620
Right. It's just a meaningless pile.

6
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:22,260
Exactly. And that is exactly what a digital service looks like when it's built from just a disconnected list of data fields.

7
00:00:22,840 --> 00:00:26,220
So today, we are exploring a fundamental shift for your ministry,

8
00:00:26,860 --> 00:00:31,280
moving away from that list and designing screens organically based on a story.

9
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:38,940
Because, um, when digital systems lack that story, the resulting services are fragmented, siloed, and duplicated.

10
00:00:39,580 --> 00:00:42,620
Yeah. And then users just have to keep entering the same things over and over.

11
00:00:42,860 --> 00:00:50,460
Precisely. Data is rekeyed by users, systems become locked into one supplier, and ultimately, they are costly to change.

12
00:00:51,060 --> 00:00:56,340
By working a screen out from a story instead, you dictate exactly where every single value comes from.

13
00:00:57,060 --> 00:00:59,580
So if the goal is to map these screens to a story,

14
00:01:00,220 --> 00:01:05,500
how does a system builder actually decide when a specific step in a user's journey warrants its own screen?

15
00:01:06,140 --> 00:01:09,660
I mean, it seems like it'd be easy to just keep adding pages endlessly.

16
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,160
Well, you prevent that by walking the story step by step and applying three strict rules for screen creation.

17
00:01:15,620 --> 00:01:21,420
Rule number one: a step where the person asks, states, gives, or confirms something gets a screen.

18
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:23,460
Okay, that makes sense. What's the second one?

19
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:30,540
Rule number two: a step that is wholly the system's gets a screen only if the person must read something before going on.

20
00:01:30,920 --> 00:01:34,300
Wait, so if they don't explicitly need to read anything from the system...

21
00:01:34,780 --> 00:01:37,280
Then no screen, which leads right into rule number three:

22
00:01:37,940 --> 00:01:41,600
two steps share a screen when nothing the system does stands between them.

23
00:01:42,140 --> 00:01:43,340
I hear those three rules, but,

24
00:01:43,980 --> 00:01:48,940
you know, in the real world, government agencies always want to collect extra data just in case they need it later.

25
00:01:49,640 --> 00:01:53,120
How do you realistically stop that data bloat once the screen is justified?

26
00:01:53,580 --> 00:01:56,640
Uh, you put up a strict firewall around what goes on the screen itself.

27
00:01:57,020 --> 00:02:01,680
There are four definitive sources for any value, and a data point must fall into one of them.

28
00:02:02,140 --> 00:02:04,340
Source one: it is taken from another body.

29
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:06,340
Right, like an external database.

30
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:07,140
Exactly.

31
00:02:07,780 --> 00:02:11,920
Source two: it is chosen from a shared list kept once for the whole service.

32
00:02:12,440 --> 00:02:13,340
Okay, and the third?

33
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:18,380
Source three: it is read from a record or a setting the authority already keeps.

34
00:02:19,140 --> 00:02:25,900
And source four: it is entered right there, typed by the person, but accompanied by a strict reason why no other place has it.

35
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:33,100
If those four sources are the only valid ways to collect data, how does a ministry actually build a screen without breaking those rules?

36
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:34,240
Let's, um,

37
00:02:35,460 --> 00:02:37,220
ground this in a concrete demonstration.

38
00:02:37,760 --> 00:02:40,780
Sure. We can look at the fictional country of Progressa.

39
00:02:40,980 --> 00:02:41,560
Progressa.

40
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:46,560
Their higher education authority has a goal for users to apply for a provisional license.

41
00:02:47,160 --> 00:02:48,960
That journey has exactly five screens.

42
00:02:49,700 --> 00:02:52,000
Only five for a whole license application?

43
00:02:52,240 --> 00:02:52,860
Just five.

44
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:59,560
Screen one asks to apply. Screen two shows the authority's standards and asks for the kind of institution.

45
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:03,980
Screen three takes the particulars and also handles two potential failures.

46
00:03:04,520 --> 00:03:05,480
Oh, like errors.

47
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:08,460
Right, like a document left out or a name already held.

48
00:03:08,920 --> 00:03:11,540
Then screen four confirms the details and takes the fee.

49
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:15,300
And finally, screen five shows the application has been received.

50
00:03:15,980 --> 00:03:21,800
The mapping between those five screens and your four rules for data sources is, like, incredibly precise.

51
00:03:22,580 --> 00:03:27,560
The applicant's name isn't typed in. It is taken directly from the national identity sign-in.

52
00:03:27,860 --> 00:03:28,680
Which is source one.

53
00:03:29,120 --> 00:03:29,320
Right.

54
00:03:30,060 --> 00:03:32,640
And the kind of institution is chosen from a shared list.

55
00:03:32,980 --> 00:03:33,600
That's source two.

56
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:37,760
The application fee is read directly from an internal finance setting.

57
00:03:38,220 --> 00:03:42,940
Source three. And the only thing entered directly by the user is the proposed name of the institution,

58
00:03:43,620 --> 00:03:46,500
because literally no other body holds that information yet.

59
00:03:46,860 --> 00:03:50,060
Yes, source four. And this aligns perfectly with the builder's perspective.

60
00:03:50,320 --> 00:03:53,940
The GovStack registration specification describes this exact workflow.

61
00:03:54,100 --> 00:04:00,900
Oh, really? Yeah. An analyst creates a guide, the applicant's form, the document upload, the payment, and a send screen.

62
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:07,480
Every field has a type and a rule for whether it must be filled, and those shared lists are reusable across all services.

63
00:04:07,980 --> 00:04:10,420
So this brings up a critical quality control check.

64
00:04:10,980 --> 00:04:16,100
When Progressa's education authority was drafting that third screen, the one taking the particulars,

65
00:04:16,740 --> 00:04:20,140
they initially included a field asking for the applicant's phone number.

66
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:23,220
And that is exactly where the framework proves its value.

67
00:04:23,660 --> 00:04:26,500
When the builder checked that field against the story, it failed.

68
00:04:26,920 --> 00:04:28,420
Because it didn't fit the sources.

69
00:04:28,820 --> 00:04:34,900
Right. No shared list named a phone number, and the national identity sign-in system does not release phone numbers.

70
00:04:35,440 --> 00:04:37,060
That meant the user would have to type it in.

71
00:04:37,460 --> 00:04:39,880
But source four requires a strict reason.

72
00:04:40,220 --> 00:04:46,240
Precisely. It prompted a necessary question to the registrar: "Why are we actually asking for this?

73
00:04:46,460 --> 00:04:49,020
Does the authority truly need to telephone an applicant?"

74
00:04:49,460 --> 00:04:53,320
And because the field lacked a valid story source, it was struck out immediately.

75
00:04:53,680 --> 00:04:59,000
That is the ultimate test. Point at any value on any screen and ask where it comes from.

76
00:04:59,320 --> 00:05:01,680
It must answer with one of those four sources.

77
00:05:02,280 --> 00:05:04,520
Anything else is a question for the story's owner.

